<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive launcher icon.

  This lives in `mipmap-anydpi`, not `mipmap-anydpi-v26`. Adaptive icons arrived
  in API 26, which is exactly this app's minSdk, so the version qualifier could
  never select anything else - and Lint says so. Keeping it would also imply the
  project supports a pre-adaptive path that it does not, and therefore needs
  bitmap fallbacks it does not have.

  All three layers are rasters, not vectors: the mark is a piece of brand art
  rather than a glyph. They are written at five densities by
  `scripts/install-icon-layers.ps1` from a delivered layer set - a background
  plate, a foreground with the safe zone already composed, and a silhouette drawn
  by the artist. `scripts/make-icons.ps1` is the other path: one mark image,
  composed into an icon here. See `branding/README.md` for which one to run.

  The background is a bitmap rather than a colour because the plate is a gradient
  with a glass disc on it, not a flat fill. The foreground keeps its own
  transparency over that plate, so the two layers cannot disagree about the mark.

  `<monochrome>` is the layer Android 13 and later tint instead of drawing the
  coloured icon, which is what makes the launcher icon follow the system theme. It
  is a real silhouette drawn as part of the artwork - white on a transparent
  ground - rather than a threshold taken of the coloured mark: this art has white
  markings inside it, and a filter would keep those as holes.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
